best sellers list on Amazon. So, I'm gonna unpack&nbsp;
3 tools that we share in this book that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.040
gonna say encapsulates The Road Trip Method.
So, by the end of this video, what's in my brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.160
about how to create conversations that matter&nbsp;
and how to bring up new topics in conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.520
and keep a conversation filled with life and&nbsp;
energy and curiosity as opposed to this like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:40.240
trailing, ending conversation with like, "So.." right?&nbsp;
And the attempts of avoiding that- and avoiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.320
that and maybe even more importantly, creating&nbsp;
conversations that are really valuable to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:55.520
and to the people that you're talking with. Number&nbsp;
1 is listen to the music behind the words, right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:03.840
So often when people say things, right, we take them&nbsp;
at face value. But, we're not a cop or a lawyer or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.360
anybody else. We don't need to just hear the facts,&nbsp;
right. We can hear the depth behind what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.640
somebody's saying. And so, some specific ways that&nbsp;
you can hear music behind the words because just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.200
generally you could take that idea and skip to&nbsp;
the next section in the video, right. Just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.600
mindset of listening to the music behind the words&nbsp;
just recognizing that if somebody shows you this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.840
what they're actually saying is all filled&nbsp;
in the space in between as well. And so, it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.840
unstated feelings that are happening. it's the&nbsp;
opinion that they're not quite sharing but is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.240
sort of embedded, it's the process of how the&nbsp;
conversation is going, right. So, I work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:47.840
a lot of leaders that are very like me to some&nbsp;
extent, ADHD busy. And so, walking into their office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.320
or hop on with them on Zoom and they're clearly&nbsp;
doing 17 other things, right. So, music behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.520
words. They might be saying something but there&nbsp;
I can see all of this other stuff going on there.&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.800
So, listening to music behind the words increases&nbsp;
the amount of data that you get in a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.000
by at least 20 percent. Sort of an arbitrary number,&nbsp;
but thinking about road trips having,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.280
you know, constant novel data coming at you at&nbsp;
70 miles per hour, listening to the music behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.760
the words increases the level of data that's in&nbsp;
the conversation. You've just got to tune into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.200
right. Oftentimes we find what we're looking for&nbsp;
and we hear what we're listening for. So, if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.240
just listening for the facts, you're just going to&nbsp;
hear the facts. If you're just listening for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.120
person to be annoying, that's all you're going to&nbsp;
hear. But, if you listen for their strengths, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.160
fears, their aspirations, the background, the whole&nbsp;
story, you're going to have a very, very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.800
conversation. Part number 2 of the road trip&nbsp;
method is you have to believe that conversation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.720
exponential. And what I mean by that is if you&nbsp;
ask- so, 1 of the 4 questions that often gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.280
asked in conversation when you first meet a person&nbsp;
like where are you from, what do you do, how are you.&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.760
Well, maybe just 3. And so, if you ask&nbsp;
"Where are you?" and that person says- where are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.120
you? Oh my goodness, where are you from is a better&nbsp;
way of phrasing that. So, if you ask a person, "Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:14.240
are you from?" And they say "Massachusetts," right.&nbsp;
That's it. That is true. I am from Massachusetts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.920
but I don't live in Massachusetts, but I am&nbsp;
from there. I live in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.200
And my social security number and address is just&nbsp;
kidding! If they just share Massachusetts, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.240
even though they only gave you 1 word of data,&nbsp;
you might be able to tell were they excited to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.880
show that they were from Massachusetts? Were they&nbsp;
completely indifferent? Did they share it in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.960
of like. "I'm from Massachusetts," right.&nbsp;
Was there- is there hesitation? So, there's that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.160
data. There's also just everything that you&nbsp;
know about Massachusetts and your experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.320
with Massachusetts, and when I say conversation is&nbsp;
exponential, even though they share just 1 word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.520
the way they feel about Massachusetts, what&nbsp;
you're curious about Massachusetts and the prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:01.360
experiences that you've had in Massachusetts are&nbsp;
3, of many probably, but just 3 potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.640
pathways that you can continue on. So, if you&nbsp;
continue on this pathway, right, there may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.160
3 others that come out. And then 3 others&nbsp;
and then 3 others and then others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.360
And this is what I mean when I say, "Conversation&nbsp;
is exponential." The problem is when we're in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.600
moment and we're wondering how to bring up new&nbsp;
topics in conversation or how to continue a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.360
really meaningful discussion, if we get&nbsp;
stuck at all, we get stuck and we think, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:32.160
there's nothing left to talk about." So, if I'm&nbsp;
on a date with my wife, Kate, and conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.680
is going, going, going and then that topic sort&nbsp;
of just kind of fizzles or dies, we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:43.840
continue that dialogue in 2 very different&nbsp;
ways if I believe that conversation is exponential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.000
versus if I think "Oh, there's nothing left to&nbsp;
talk about," right. We've been married for 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.080
years. We've talked about everything under the&nbsp;
sun that we can possibly talk about anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:00.720
else. That lit- that is an extremely limiting&nbsp;
belief that will prevent topic exploration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.760
in conversation, whereas if I say even though we've&nbsp;
been talking for 30 minutes, and so I believe if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:12.800
someone spoke for 30 minutes, you have at least&nbsp;
300 different pathways of potential conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.880
if you're listening to the music behind&nbsp;
the words. And then the 3rd idea here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:23.040
comes directly from Ask Powerful Questions and it&nbsp;
is this idea of asking reflective questions. So, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.320
can you know just pull questions out of thin air,&nbsp;
out of your own curiosity. Reflective questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.680
is a little bit different. It's where you reflect&nbsp;
